const faqs = [
  { q: "What is Chinese mythology?", a: "Not one tidy religion, but a vast, layered web of stories — creation myths, gods and immortals, monsters, ghosts, rebel heroes and tragic lovers — drawn from ancient legend, folk belief, Daoism, Buddhism and classic novels, retold and reshaped over thousands of years." },
  { q: "Is Chinese mythology like Greek mythology?", a: "They rhyme but don't match. Both have gods, monsters and heroes, but Greek myth centers on a fixed pantheon on Mount Olympus, while Chinese myth is more like a sprawling bureaucracy — heaven runs like a government, immortals earn their rank, and many 'gods' were once humans who ascended." },
  { q: "Who is the most famous Chinese mythological figure?", a: "Sun Wukong, the Monkey King — a stone-born trickster who declared war on heaven and is now known worldwide through Journey to the West. Nezha, the rebel child-god, is a close second." },
  { q: "Are Chinese dragons evil?", a: "No — and this is the single biggest misunderstanding. Chinese dragons (龙) are benevolent: they bring rain and rivers, symbolize the emperor and good fortune, and are something to be proud to be born under. They are nothing like the fire-breathing villains of Western tales." },
  { q: "What is heaven in Chinese mythology?", a: "Less a paradise, more a celestial government. 天庭 (the Heavenly Court) has an emperor, ministers, ranks and paperwork. A god's power comes with an official post — which is exactly what makes Sun Wukong's refusal to 'know his place' so radical." },
  { q: "Are Chinese spirits demons?", a: "Not necessarily. A 妖 (yao) is a spirit or shapeshifter — often an animal or object that gained power over time. Some are dangerous, but many are good, wise, even heroic. The White Snake is a yao who gives up immortality for love. Translating yao as 'demon' loses all of that." },
  { q: "Where should a beginner start?", a: "Start with Sun Wukong for rebellion, Chang'e for a tragic moon myth, and the Classic of Mountains and Seas for monsters. Those three open three different doors into the whole world." },
  { q: "Are these stories still important in China today?", a: "Very. They live in festivals, idioms, films, games and everyday speech. A modern blockbuster about Nezha can break box-office records — these aren't dusty legends, they're a living culture." },
];
